      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "INTRODUCTION",
          "content": "Adjuvant antibiotic therapy for acute abdominal conditions is widely used. Its timing, duration, dose and spectrum, however, are not homogeneous amongst surgeons and prolonged courses are often used despite the unproven benefits of this practice."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "To evaluate use and compare duration of antibiotic treatments in acute abdominal surgery."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "Retrospective cohort study. The medical records of 290 patients who underwent operations for acute abdomen from July 1998 to July 1999 in a teaching hospital were reviewed. The pattern of antibiotic use and rates of postoperative complications were evaluated, along with surgical diagnosis, degree of contamination/infection, and incidence of postoperative complications. The patients were stratified according to the degree of contamination/infection noted during the operation. The study population was divided in two groups according to the duration of antibiotic use (cut-off point at the median antibiotic use in days, for each group of contamination/infection degree), and outcomes were compared."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "The degree of contamination/infection was significantly associated with an increased risk of wound infection, intra-abdominal abscess, postoperative infective complications and overall postoperative complications (p < 0.001). A long course of antibiotics was not associated with lower infective complication rates."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "Shorter courses of antibiotic therapy based on the degree of contamination/infection seem to be safe. A prospective study should confirm this hypothesis."
        }
      ]},
